<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:luna="clr-namespace:EmuSen.LunaP.Controls">

    <!-- A header row above a list, sharing its column widths with every row in
         it - see docs/LunaP.md §27.

         Grid.IsSharedSizeScope is what makes an Auto column line up: the header
         grid and each row grid are separate grids, so without a shared scope an
         Auto column would size itself independently in every one of them and
         nothing would align. The scope goes on the root so both parts are
         inside it.

         This half is necessary and not sufficient, and the other half is not
         here. A definition only actually joins the scope if it is ADDED to a
         grid's own collection rather than assigned as a ready-made one - see
         LunaTable.Define, and docs/LunaP.md §27.7. Setting the scope here and
         getting that wrong leaves a control that looks correct everywhere
         except on screen, which is how it shipped.

         The header's padding matches the item container's below, because those
         two numbers are what actually decide whether a column heading sits over
         its own cells.

         `:is(...)` AND NOT A BARE TYPE SELECTOR, which is the whole reason the
         first draft of this style did nothing at all. An Avalonia type selector
         is an EXACT match: `luna|LunaTable` selects instances whose type is
         precisely LunaTable, and every instance there will ever be is a
         LunaTable<T> deriving from it. The symptom is the one §5.5 keeps
         recording - a control with no template, no error, and nothing on
         screen. Every other style in the kit names a type that is instantiated
         directly, which is why the trap has not appeared elsewhere. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable)">
        <Setter Property="Grid.IsSharedSizeScope" Value="True" />
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <ControlTemplate>
                <DockPanel>
                    <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top"
                            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource LunaBorder}"
                            BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
                            Padding="12,4,12,6">
                        <Grid Name="PART_Header" />
                    </Border>
                    <ListBox Name="PART_Rows"
                             Background="Transparent"
                             BorderThickness="0" />
                </DockPanel>
            </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- A SORTABLE HEADING IS A BUTTON WEARING NOTHING. LunaTable.Heading makes
         one for any column with a comparison, because a heading that answers
         only a click is a sort a keyboard user does not have (§24) - a Button
         brings focus, Tab, Space, Enter and an invoke peer for free.

         What it also brings is Fluent's chrome, and all of this is taking it
         off again: the heading has to sit on the same baseline and the same x
         as the plain TextBlock in the column beside it, or a table with one
         sortable column among three has a heading row that steps up and down.

         Background is set on the ContentPresenter and not on the Button. Fluent
         paints a Button's fill in its template, so a Setter on the control is
         overridden by the theme the moment a pointer enters; the pointerover and
         pressed rules below reach the presenter, which is what actually draws. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Button.heading">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Button.heading /template/ ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The hover affordance, and the only one: a heading that looks identical
         whether it sorts or not gives a mouse user nothing to discover. The
         glyph tells them afterwards; this is what tells them before. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Button.heading:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) Button.heading:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource LunaInputSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The sort direction. Hidden entirely in the third state rather than shown
         as a neutral mark, so the cycle reads as two sorted states and off. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) TextBlock.sort">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource LunaInfo}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The other half of the alignment. Fluent's ListBoxItem padding is not the
         header's, so left alone the columns start at two different x positions;
         this pins both to 12. The vertical figure is smaller than Fluent's
         default because a table is read as rows of data rather than as a menu
         of choices, and Fluent's spacing puts four fields on a screen. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(luna|LunaTable) ListBoxItem">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,4" />
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
    </Style>

</Styles>
